    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Muslim sultanate at the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a> that rose after the Portuguese took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511 and dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a> trade.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> (1607–1636) it conquered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> but lost its fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a> in 1629.</li><li>Its scholars made it a centre of Malay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>, and it fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> from 1873 until the sultan surrendered in 1903.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Four queens ruled Aceh in succession from 1641 to 1699, beginning with Taj ul-Alam Safiatuddin Syah, daughter of the conqueror <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

