    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#banda-aceh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banda-aceh.jpg" alt="Banda Aceh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#banda-aceh">Banda Aceh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meuseujid_Raya_Baiturrahman,_Aceh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Si Gam, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aceh grew up at the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> opens onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, an area earlier dominated by the port-kingdom of Lamuri and by Samudera Pasai, one of the first Muslim states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Later Acehnese tradition dates the founding of the sultanate to 1496, but its real rise began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultan">Sultan</a> Ali Mughayat Syah, who ruled from about 1514 until 1530. The Portuguese conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511 transformed the region: Muslim merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a> and the Malay world sought ports outside Portuguese control, and Aceh was well placed to attract them. Ali Mughayat Syah united the small kingdoms of the north coast, conquering Daya, Pidie and in 1524 Pasai, and drove out the Portuguese, who had built a fort at Pasai and supported the ruler of Pidie. His capital, Bandar Aceh Darussalam near the mouth of the Aceh River, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#banda-aceh">Kutaraja</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> and now Banda Aceh, became the seat of a dynasty that identified itself firmly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>. Within a generation Aceh had become the leading Muslim power of northern Sumatra and the chief rival of Portuguese Malacca for the trade of the strait, drawing Muslim shipping that had once called at Malacca to its own harbour.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mecca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mecca.jpg" alt="Mecca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mecca">Mecca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_Mecca1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saudipics.com, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aceh's prosperity rested above all on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a>. Grown on the coasts of northern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a>, it was shipped through Aceh to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gujarat">Gujarat</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the Ottoman lands by a route that bypassed the Portuguese, and later sold to Chinese, English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> and French buyers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> the crown tried to monopolise the pepper trade of the conquered west-coast ports. Gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#camphor">camphor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benzoin-resin">benzoin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> were also traded, and the capital was a cosmopolitan port of Gujarati, Tamil, Arab, Turkish, Chinese and Malay merchants. Society was divided between the court and its servants, a wealthy merchant aristocracy, the territorial chiefs known as <em>uleebalang</em>, who controlled villages and river valleys, and the Islamic scholars (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulama">ulama</a></em>), who ran religious schools and held great influence over village life. Wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> farming in the Aceh valley fed the capital, and captives taken in war worked in the city and on the land. Aceh became a major centre of Islamic learning and the port of embarkation for pilgrims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mecca">Mecca</a>, earning the name <em>Serambi Mekkah</em>, 'the Veranda of Mecca'. In the nineteenth century a new pepper boom on the west coast, led by local chiefs, made Aceh, by some estimates, the source of about half the world's pepper supply in the 1820s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#aceh-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aceh-war.jpg" alt="Aceh War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#aceh-war">Aceh War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samalanga_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Geschilderd naar Jules Garnier (Collectie Koninklijk Huisarchief in bruikleen aan museum Bronbeek), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aceh was among the strongest military powers of maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Its army included infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephants</a> and a fleet of large oared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galley">galleys</a>, and from the 1560s it obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">cannon</a>, gunners and military experts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> al-Kahhar had approached for help against the Portuguese. Aceh attacked Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malacca">Malacca</a> repeatedly between 1537 and 1629 without success, and fought a long three-way struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#johor">Johor</a> for control of the strait. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> (r. 1607–1636) built the most formidable force: European visitors described hundreds of galleys and large numbers of elephants. He conquered Deli and Aru in 1612–1613, sacked Johor's capital Batu Sawar in 1613, and took Pahang, Kedah and Perak between 1617 and 1620, carrying off thousands of captives to his capital. In 1629 his great armada besieged Malacca, but a Portuguese fleet under Nuno Álvares Botelho, with help from Johor and Patani, destroyed it, a defeat from which Aceh's offensive power never recovered. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aceh-war">Aceh War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> from 1873, Acehnese fighters defended fortified villages and later waged a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war under leaders such as Teungku Chik di Tiro, Teuku Umar and Cut Nyak Dhien, inflicting heavy losses on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> colonial army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda.jpg" alt="Iskandar Muda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lukisan_Iskandar_muda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultan ruled from his palace (<em>dalam</em>) in the capital, with power that varied greatly from reign to reign. Strong rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> curbed the wealthy merchant aristocrats, the <em>orang kaya</em>, through confiscations and executions, monopolised trade and governed conquered ports through appointed officials. Under weaker rulers the orang kaya and the uleebalang held real power. Islamic law was administered by a chief judge, the <em>qadi malik al-adil</em>, and the court took pride in combining Islamic law with custom; later tradition attributed Aceh's customary constitution, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adat">Adat</a> Meukuta Alam</em>, to Iskandar Muda. In 1641, on the death of Iskandar Thani, his widow, Iskandar Muda's daughter, became sultana as Taj ul-Alam Safiatuddin Syah, and four queens ruled in succession until 1699. Their reigns, supported by the orang kaya, were largely peaceful and allowed trade and scholarship to flourish, though the crown lost its hold over its outlying dependencies. In 1699 the last queen was deposed and an Arab sayyid became sultan; from 1727 a dynasty of Bugis descent held the throne until 1903, presiding over a looser state in which the uleebalang and the confederations of the Aceh valley, known as <em>sagi</em>, were largely self-governing.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#strait-of-malacca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca.jpg" alt="Strait of Malacca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strait_of_Malacca_highlighted.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Derives from png uploaded by User:Wolrd blank map, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aceh shaped the history of the Malay world for three centuries. Its wars with the Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#johor">Johor</a> determined the balance of power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, and its contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> made it the Ottomans' most important partner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, a link remembered in Acehnese tradition long afterwards. Above all, Aceh was a centre of Islamic scholarship in Malay. Hamzah Fansuri wrote mystical poetry that made Malay a medium of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sufism">Sufi</a> thought; Syamsuddin of Pasai served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a> as chief scholar; Nuruddin ar-Raniri, a Gujarati scholar at the court of Iskandar Thani, wrote the encyclopaedic <em>Bustan al-Salatin</em> and attacked the mystical teachings of his predecessors; and Abdurrauf of Singkil, chief judge under the queens, produced the first complete Malay commentary on the Qur'an. Texts written or copied in Aceh spread across the archipelago. The court chronicle <em>Hikayat Aceh</em> and the poem of holy war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a>, <em>Hikayat Prang Sabi</em>, belong to its literary heritage. The memory of independence and of long resistance to the Dutch remained central to Acehnese identity, feeding the Darul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a> revolt of the 1950s and the Free Aceh Movement, whose conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indonesia">Indonesia</a> ended in the Helsinki peace agreement of 2005, after which Aceh gained special autonomy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultanate's independence was preserved for a time by rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> bound the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> to respect Aceh's independence, but disputes over piracy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a> trade, and fears that another power might gain a foothold, led Britain to give the Netherlands a free hand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a> Treaty of 1871. In March 1873 the Dutch declared war. Their first expedition was repulsed and its commander, General J.H.R. Köhler, killed; a second, larger force under General Jan van Swieten took the sultan's palace in January 1874, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sultan">Sultan</a> Mahmud Syah died of cholera soon afterwards. The Acehnese proclaimed the child Muhammad Daud Syah sultan, and the leadership of resistance passed to the uleebalang and especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulama">ulama</a>, who preached holy war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aceh-war">Aceh War</a> became one of the longest and costliest colonial conflicts, and for years the Dutch held little more than a fortified line around the capital. On the advice of the Islamic scholar Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje, they eventually allied with the uleebalang against the ulama, and from 1898 General J.B. van Heutsz pursued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> with mobile units of the Marechaussee. Sultan Muhammad Daud Syah surrendered in January 1903, ending the sultanate, though fighting continued in the interior for years and tens of thousands had died.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Aceh Sultanate, the leading Muslim power of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a> from the sixteenth century, controlled much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepper">pepper</a> trade, fought the Portuguese for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, became a centre of Malay Islamic scholarship and resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> conquest for thirty years until 1903.</em></p>

